Divide to obtain the slope
If the \(x_i\) are not all identical, then \(\sum_iX_i^2&gt;0\). Divide both sides:
\[
\boxed{
b_1=\frac{\sum_iX_iY_i}{\sum_iX_i^2}
=\frac{\sum_i(x_i-\bar x)(y_i-\bar y)}
       {\sum_i(x_i-\bar x)^2}
}.
\]

Numerator: how \(x\) and \(y\) vary together. Denominator: how much \(x\) varies. If \(x\) has no variation, a slope cannot be estimated.

By the end you can

identify the independent observational unit;
calculate and report a paired effect in feet;
construct and interpret a sign-flip null distribution;
fit and diagnose a simple linear groundwater trend; and
write conclusions that state evidence without claiming a cause.
